# 🐍 Python OOPs – Day 90

## 🔐 Topic: Encapsulation – Getters, Setters & Properties

Today I continued learning **Encapsulation** in Python.

Encapsulation is the process of **bundling data and the methods that work on that data together inside a class**, while controlling how the data is accessed and modified.

Previously, I learned about public, protected, private members and name mangling. Today I learned how getters, setters and properties are used to work with private attributes.

---

## 🔹 1. Getter

A **getter** is a method used to **read or get the value** of an attribute.

It is commonly used when working with private attributes.

```python
class Student:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.__name = name

    def get_name(self):
        return self.__name


s1 = Student("Namratha")

print(s1.get_name())

Output:

Namratha

Here, __name is a private attribute and get_name() is used to access its value.

Key Point

Getter → Used to get/read a value

🔹 2. Setter

A setter is a method used to modify or change the value of an attribute.

class Student:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.__name = name

    def get_name(self):
        return self.__name

    def set_name(self, name):
        self.__name = name


s1 = Student("Namratha")

print(s1.get_name())

s1.set_name("Anu")

print(s1.get_name())

Output:

Namratha
Anu
Key Point

Setter → Used to set/change a value

A setter can also be used to control or validate the value before modifying the attribute.

🔹 3. @property

The @property decorator allows a method to be accessed like an attribute.

Without @property:

print(s1.get_name())

With @property:

print(s1.name)

Example:

class Student:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.__name = name

    @property
    def name(self):
        return self.__name


s1 = Student("Namratha")

print(s1.name)

Output:

Namratha

The name() method is treated like an attribute:

s1.name

instead of:

s1.name()
Key Point

@property → Makes a method behave like an attribute when accessed

🔹 4. Property Setter

A property setter combines @property with a setter.

It allows us to modify a value using normal attribute assignment.

class Student:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.__name = name

    @property
    def name(self):
        return self.__name

    @name.setter
    def name(self, value):
        self.__name = value


s1 = Student("Namratha")

print(s1.name)

s1.name = "Anu"

print(s1.name)

Output:

Namratha
Anu

Here:

s1.name = "Anu"

sets the value through the property setter.

Key Point

@name.setter → Used to set/change the value through a property

🔹 5. Property Deleter

A property deleter is used to define what happens when a property is deleted.

class Student:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.__name = name

    @property
    def name(self):
        return self.__name

    @name.setter
    def name(self, value):
        self.__name = value

    @name.deleter
    def name(self):
        del self.__name

Now:

del s1.name

will call the property deleter.

Key Point

@name.deleter → Used to delete the value through a property

🧠 Quick Summary
Concept	Purpose
Getter	Read/get a value
Setter	Modify/change a value
@property	Access a method like an attribute
Property setter	Set a value through a property
Property deleter	Delete a value through a property
Easy Memory Trick
